      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Mechanism of injury**: Fall on outstretched hand (FOOSH) — acute traumatic tear vs. acute-on-chronic. Did he have any pre-existing shoulder symptoms? Chronic degenerative tears are common at this age.\n- **Workers\' compensation status**: Was this an on-the-job injury? Workers\' comp claim filed? This will affect treatment pathway, authorization, and return-to-work planning.\n- **Dominant hand involvement**: Right-dominant with right shoulder tear — higher functional impact.\n- **Occupational demands**: Warehouse manager — overhead lifting, carrying, pushing/pulling requirements. Quantify physical demands (sedentary, light, medium, heavy, very heavy per DOT classification).\n- **Prior shoulder history**: Any previous injury, injection, or treatment to the right shoulder. Prior imaging for comparison.\n- **Smoking status**: Smoking impairs tendon healing and increases re-tear rates. Critical for surgical planning.\n- **Medical comorbidities**: Diabetes (impairs healing), rheumatoid arthritis, osteoporosis (anchor fixation concerns), anticoagulation.\n- **Functional limitations**: Specific ADL limitations — dressing, bathing, grooming, sleeping, driving, and work activities.\n- **Pain management**: Current pain medications, effectiveness, any opioid use since injury.\n- **Patient expectations and preferences**: Understanding of diagnosis, desire for surgery vs. conservative management, timeline expectations.',
