    const Section(
      title: '+ Classify this rotator cuff tear and discuss the indications for surgical versus conservative management.',
      content: '**Rotator Cuff Tear Classification**:\n\n**By Thickness**:\n- **Partial-thickness**: Articular-sided, bursal-sided, or intratendinous. Graded by depth (<25%, 25-50%, >50% of tendon thickness).\n- **Full-thickness**: Complete disruption from bursal to articular surface. This patient has a full-thickness tear.\n\n**By Size (full-thickness, based on retraction)**:\n- Small: <1 cm\n- Medium: 1-3 cm\n- Large: 3-5 cm\n- Massive: >5 cm or involving 2+ tendons\n- **This patient**: 2.5 cm retraction — **medium-sized** full-thickness tear\n\n**By Chronicity**:\n- Acute: Traumatic event, no pre-existing changes\n- Chronic: Degenerative, fatty infiltration, muscle atrophy\n- **Acute-on-chronic**: Pre-existing degeneration with acute traumatic extension — likely in this 58-year-old given mild fatty infiltration\n\n**Goutallier Classification (Fatty Infiltration on MRI/CT)**:\n- Grade 0: No fat\n- Grade 1: Some fatty streaks (this patient)\n- Grade 2: Fat < muscle\n- Grade 3: Fat = muscle\n- Grade 4: Fat > muscle\n- Grades 3-4 predict poor surgical outcomes and irreversible muscle changes.\n\n**Indications FAVORING Surgical Repair**:\n1. **Acute traumatic full-thickness tear** in a functional, active individual — strongest indication\n2. Full-thickness tear with significant functional impairment\n3. Failure of 6-12 weeks of conservative management\n4. Younger, active patient (<65) with high physical demands\n5. Tear retraction <3 cm with Goutallier Grade 0-2 (repairable with good tissue quality)\n6. Workers\' compensation with heavy labor demands\n\n**Indications FAVORING Conservative Management**:\n1. Partial-thickness tears (especially <50% depth)\n2. Chronic, massive, retracted tears with Grade 3-4 fatty infiltration (poor surgical outcomes)\n3. Low-demand, elderly patients (>70-75) with minimal functional impairment\n4. Significant medical comorbidities making surgery high-risk\n5. Patient preference after informed discussion\n6. Intact or compensated shoulder function despite imaging findings\n\n**For This Patient**: Multiple factors favor surgical referral — acute traumatic mechanism, dominant extremity, medium-sized tear with minimal fatty infiltration (good tissue quality, better prognosis), high occupational demands, and age (58 — reasonable surgical candidate). However, a trial of conservative management for 6-12 weeks is still reasonable if the patient prefers, as some full-thickness tears compensate well with rehabilitation. The key is early surgical consultation so the option is not delayed — chronic retraction and progressive fatty infiltration reduce surgical success.',
