      content: '**Conservative Management Protocol (if chosen)**:\n\n**Phase 1 — Acute (Weeks 0-6)**:\n- Sling for comfort (not immobilization) — wean as tolerated by 2-4 weeks\n- Pain management: Ice, acetaminophen, short course of NSAIDs, tramadol if needed. Avoid opioids beyond 1-2 weeks.\n- Gentle pendulum exercises and PROM beginning immediately\n- Scapular retraction and postural exercises\n\n**Phase 2 — Recovery (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- Progressive AROM and stretching\n- Isometric rotator cuff strengthening, progressing to isotonic\n- Scapular stabilization exercises\n- Closed kinetic chain exercises (wall push-ups)\n\n**Phase 3 — Strengthening (Weeks 12-24)**:\n- Progressive resistance training for rotator cuff and scapular stabilizers\n- Functional movement retraining for work tasks\n- Deltoid compensation training (anterior deltoid can partially compensate for supraspinatus)\n\n**Reassessment**: If no meaningful improvement by 12 weeks of compliant conservative care, recommend surgical repair before further retraction and fatty infiltration occur.\n\n**Post-Surgical Rehabilitation Protocol** (after arthroscopic rotator cuff repair):\n\n**Phase 1 — Maximum Protection (Weeks 0-6)**:\n- Sling immobilization for 6 weeks (critical for tendon-to-bone healing)\n- Only passive ROM exercises performed by therapist: forward flexion to 120 degrees, external rotation to 30 degrees\n- Pendulum exercises\n- Elbow, wrist, and hand AROM to prevent stiffness\n- Scapular isometrics (retraction, depression)\n- Cryotherapy for pain and swelling\n- **No active shoulder movement. No lifting. No driving.**\n\n**Phase 2 — Active Motion (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- Wean from sling\n- Progress to active-assisted then active ROM\n- Isometric rotator cuff exercises in neutral\n- Light resistance band exercises beginning at week 8-10\n- Scapular stabilization exercises\n- May begin driving when off sling and able to safely control the steering wheel\n\n**Phase 3 — Strengthening (Weeks 12-20)**:\n- Progressive isotonic rotator cuff strengthening\n- Resistance band and light dumbbell exercises\n- Closed and open kinetic chain exercises\n- Sport/work-specific movement patterns\n- Core and kinetic chain strengthening\n\n**Phase 4 — Return to Activity (Weeks 20-26+)**:\n- Full unrestricted ROM expected\n- Strength at least 80-90% of contralateral side\n- Work hardening program for return to warehouse duties\n- Functional capacity evaluation (FCE) before return to heavy labor\n\n**Key Milestones**: Full PROM by week 8. Full AROM by week 12. Return to desk work by week 8-10. Return to heavy labor by 6-9 months (with FCE clearance). Full recovery up to 12 months.',
